CodeBrix patch 0003 - ErrorFilter.Internal error scopes (wgpu-native/src/lib.rs)

WHAT: wgpuDevicePushErrorScope accepts WGPUErrorFilter_Internal (it panicked - a process abort at
the C boundary - for anything but Validation / OutOfMemory). handle_error classifies an error as
internal when wgpu-core's own WebGpuError classification says ErrorType::Internal (shader
generation failures; backend pipeline linkage / entry-point failures) and stores it as a new
Error::Internal, captured by the innermost Internal scope, reported as WGPUErrorType_Internal by
wgpuDevicePopErrorScope, or delivered to the uncaptured-error callback when no Internal scope is
open. The same classification also fixes OutOfMemory: wgpu-core wraps DeviceError with
`#[error(transparent)]`, which forwards source() PAST it, so the source() walk never saw a wrapped
DeviceError::OutOfMemory and reported it as a VALIDATION error (observed: an over-large texture,
"Not enough memory left.", captured by a Validation scope). Validation and device-lost handling are
unchanged (device loss still needs a bare DeviceError::Lost - reclassifying wrapped losses would
run the device-lost callback, whose default ends the process, on every later call); nothing is
relabelled. The classified type list is the wgpu-core 29 error types that can classify as
Internal or OutOfMemory; an unlisted type keeps the old walk.
wgpuDevicePopErrorScope on an EMPTY stack calls back with WGPUPopErrorScopeStatus_Error (webgpu.h)
instead of panicking on unwrap().
WHY: applications open Internal scopes during normal initialization (WebGPU's GPUErrorFilter
"internal"); the engine aborted the process on the push.
UPSTREAM: no fix exists. gfx-rs/wgpu-native trunk (589e6caa4e, 2026-10-05) still panics on the
filter; open PR #630 ("Expand wgpu 30 API parity") classifies through webgpu_error_type() but maps
Internal onto Validation and still panics on the filter. CodeBrix's own change; candidate for
upstreaming.

